00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:25.189
Last way to think about density.

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:27.240
So the first way was to
think of it as a formula.

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:30.910
The second way is to use
it as a conversion factor.

00:17:31.379 --> 00:17:34.990
This is one of the few conversion
terms that you have that allows

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:37.129
you to go from mass to volume.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:38.770
It's a bridge.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:42.460
It allows you to convert from
one type of unit to another.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:46.620
It allows you to derive new values
based on things you can find in the lab.

00:17:47.229 --> 00:17:52.499
So, if I, let's say I have water, right,
which is one gram per mL is its density.

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:59.250
We can write this as a conversion term by
saying that, okay, one gram is equivalent

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:05.439
to one milliliter, like a fraction, or
we could flip it to use it for however

00:18:05.439 --> 00:18:07.779
you want to cancel out units, right?

00:18:07.779 --> 00:18:13.260
So if I had one ml, you could also say
